					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tW2BmLeFUsCPX2XwQ",
					"name": "Chitin (Lictor)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 60, all locations. Weak Points 5 (as a Ravener's or Warrior's, matching framework.md's own figure for this tier): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 5 or less halves DR (30). Eye clusters run at DR 30 rather than 60.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 60,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 60
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tILbUb4QYvaOG9o0H",
					"name": "Chameleonic Hide",
					"reference": "B41",
					"local_notes": "-4 to others' Vision rolls to spot the Lictor while it is motionless, or -2 while moving (per Basic's Chameleon rule, B41); does not work against Infravision, scent-based tracking, or other non-visual detection.\n\nLore: A Lictor's hide shifts colour and texture to match its surroundings well enough to stand motionless in plain sight and go unnoticed until it strikes — the bioform's whole reason for existing in Codex background, and the source of its reputation as the swarm's perfect assassin in Black Library's Hive Fleet fiction.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Chameleon (B41/P43, 5 points/level), level 4, 20 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 4
				},
				{
					"id": "ta6J1XPN6Xemz7ZMb",
					"name": "Pheromone Trail",
					"local_notes": "Automatically marks the Lictor's path and kill sites with a chemical trail other Tyranid organisms can follow without a roll, for as long as roughly a day; the GM may use this to have the nearest swarm elements arrive at a Lictor's last known location once its scouting or killing is done.\n\nLore: Lictors scout ahead of a landing and leave a chemical trail for the rest of the Hive Fleet to follow to a viable kill zone — standard Codex background on the bioform's actual battlefield purpose (infiltration and target-marking, not sustained combat).\n\nDesign: Perk (B21, 1 point): a narrow, always-on tracking convenience for other Tyranids rather than a combat effect, priced the same way this project's other Perk-tier flavour traits (Necron's \"Beneath a Daemon's Notice,\" this file's own Klan-style flags) are.",
					"tags": [
						"Perk",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "tzM3eE90kgEACsecr",
					"name": "Shadow in the Warp (Lictor)",
					"reference": "B35",
					"local_notes": "As the Ravener's Shadow in the Warp trait, above: 8-yard radius, -3 to psychic rolls, forced Fright Check on first exposure.\n\nLore: A Lictor is large enough to carry the same psychic weight as any other mid-tier bioform, on top of everything else that makes it a nightmare to hunt.\n\nDesign: Identical build and math to the Ravener's Shadow in the Warp trait, above (20 points).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "md8GPyL_XBwo7KrzV",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Warp-Deafened: -3 to psychic skill and Warp Empowerment-related rolls, plus an immediate Fright Check on first exposure, -30 pts)",
							"cost_adj": "30%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m16BeKc8ss2x9SfBq",
							"name": "Area Effect, 8 yards",
							"cost_adj": "+150%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mY5-sEYaBzcAtZk0B",
							"name": "Always On",
							"cost_adj": "-40%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mtbv7ddaUq46oTGZz",
							"name": "Emanation",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m2S4EV4TarOoeBbbJ",
							"name": "Accessibility (only affects psykers and other warp-sensitive minds)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tdLCbrSvft81oUwdc",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated).\n\nLore: A Lictor's dense, sinewy body keeps stalking through wounds. Large synapse-beasts and monsters shrug off wounds that would fell smaller creatures.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 2 = 50 points. Gives the Lictor the bulk-and-redundant-organs toughness the lore describes on top of its chitin DR and extra HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tdKmCARClPbvwgG8W",
			"name": "Flesh Hooks",
			"reference": "B88",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A cluster of long, backward-curved hooks grown from the forearms of Raveners and some Tyranid Primes, built less to kill outright than to snag a fleeing target or drag an objective-holder out of cover and into the swarm's reach. Codex: Tyranids background and the current tabletop's own objective-control rules for Flesh Hooks agree on this grab-and-drag role over a purely offensive one.\n\nDesign: No framework anchor names Flesh Hooks by dice, only by role (grapple and drag), so it is built as a plain natural weapon at a Ravener's own ST 22 (thr 2d) with no bonus dice bought on top, since its point is the grab, not the kill: Impaling Striker (B88, 8 points, the hooked-barb reading of a Piercing Striker's family) with the Long 1 enhancement (this project's own estimated +40%/level, as the Lash Whip above) for the extra reach the hooks need to snag at range 2: 8x1.4=11.2, rounded to 11. No exact-dice penetration table is given since Flesh Hooks are not built or priced as an armour-cracking weapon; thr imp at ST 22 (2d imp) is a believable puncture against flesh and unarmoured targets, and the grapple note above is the symbiote's actual battlefield function.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 11,
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "weyqyvtt3hwnigDwO",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "imp",
						"st": "thr"
					},
					"reach": "1,2",
					"parry": "No",
					"usage": "Standard",
					"usage_notes": "Grapple: a solid hit lets the bearer make an immediate Wrestling- or ST-based grapple attempt against the same target at no extra attack, per the GM's usual grappling rules (B370) — a house ruling on how the hooks' snag-and-drag function plays at the table, not a separately priced ability, since it only ever follows a hit this weapon's own attack roll already paid for.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": 0
						},
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Brawling"
							}
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tkdqGkkRftZoqnyaP",
			"name": "Rending Claws",
			"reference": "B88",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Genestealer's signature weapon: claws sharp and strong enough to punch clean through ceramite and plasteel on a solid hit, not merely cut flesh. Codex: Tyranids background and the Deathwatch RPG's own Genestealer material both single out rending claws as capable of shearing through power armour where an ordinary scything talon cannot — precisely the divisor-swap-on-a-good-hit this project's \"rending\" house rule (framework.md) exists to model.\n\nDesign: Framework.md's own anchor: \"Rending claws | sw+2d(3) cut; rending: a hit that succeeds by 5+ or is a critical uses armour divisor (10) instead.\" Built at a Genestealer's own ST 18 (sw 3d, framework's bioform table): Cutting Striker (B88, 7 points) with Armor Divisor (3) (B102, +100%) = 14 for the sw+0(3) baseline; the anchor's own +2d is bought as Innate Attack (Cut) (B61, 7/die) at 2 dice = 14, with Armor Divisor (3) (+100%) and Melee Attack, Reach C (-30%): net +70%, 14x1.7=23.8, rounded to 24. Total 14+24=38. The Rending hit line costs nothing extra: it is the same attack, the same dice, and the same points already paid above — only the armour divisor applied to a hit that already succeeded well changes, exactly as framework.md's own rule describes it (a chance-based crit effect, not a bought capability). Checked by exact dice distribution at ST 18 (sw+2d = 5d): the ordinary (3) line averages 17.5 and penetrates flak 96.8%, carapace (eff. 23.3) 5.9%, Sororitas (eff. 28.3) 0.08%, and 0% against Astartes plate or heavier — a solid but unremarkable melee weapon on an ordinary hit. The Rending hit's (10) line, same 5d average 17.5: flak 100%, carapace (eff. 7) 99.7%, Sororitas (eff. 8.5) 99.3%, Astartes plate (eff. 10) 96.8%, and even a Terminator (eff. 20) 22.2% — genuinely \"shears through ceramite,\" exactly what the anchor's own text claims, and only on the hits the rending rule says should count.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 38,
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "waBJC_WYyglr6kOVr",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cut",
						"st": "sw",
						"base": "2d",
						"armor_divisor": 3
					},
					"reach": "1",
					"parry": "0",
					"usage": "Standard",
					"usage_notes": "Rending: on a hit that succeeds by 5 or more, or on a critical hit, use the Rending hit line below instead of this one (roll the same damage dice, but apply divisor (10)) — framework.md's own rending rule, priced here as a conditional reinterpretation of an already-bought hit rather than a separately-purchased ability, since it never triggers without this weapon's own attack roll succeeding well in the first place.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": 0
						},
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Brawling"
							}
						}
					]
				},
